# =============================================================================
# experiment/contactangle/cont180_T-20_theta120.opts
#
# Isothermal growth/shrinkage against a saturated vapour reservoir.
#
#   cos(theta) = (sigma_as - sigma_is)/sigma_ia
#              = (0.300000 - 0.354500)/0.109000 = -0.500000  ->  theta = 120.000 deg
#
# Meniscus curvature this implies in the H = 125 um channel, chi = 2|cos th|/H:
#   R_arc = 1.250e-04 m, |chi| = 8.000e+03 1/m -- convex, chi > 0 -> ice shrinks against a reservoir
#
# VAPOUR RESERVOIR on the x-faces at saturation, both ends equal. This is the
# wedge campaign's boundary condition (-flag_BC_rhovfix 1 -rhovfix_axis 0) with
# its +/-5e-6 imbalance removed, so the imposed gradient is gone and the
# MENISCUS CURVATURE SET BY THETA is the only remaining driver.
#
# That is what makes growth observable. In a sealed box the ice can change by at
# most ~1e-6 of itself; with a reservoir the equilibrium vapour density at a
# curved interface, rho_vs(1 + d0*chi), sits below saturation when the meniscus
# is concave and above it when convex, so:
#     theta < 90  (chi < 0, concave) -> draws vapour in  -> ice GROWS
#     theta = 90  (chi = 0, flat)    -> no net flux      -> ice steady
#     theta > 90  (chi > 0, convex)  -> releases vapour  -> ice SHRINKS
#
# This needs BOTH reservoirs. Mass alone is not enough -- the latent heat has to
# go somewhere too, or the temperature moves and cancels the supersaturation.
#
# Reservoirs are on the x-faces ONLY. Pinning vapour on the y walls would feed
# ice directly at its contact line and corrupt the angle (lunar_main.c warns
# about exactly this).
#
# CONTINUATION of the 90-day grow90 run, to t = 180 days.
#
# Restart is by -initial_cond <final sol_*.dat of the first leg>, which must be
# given on the command line because the path is run-specific. -t_start 7.776e6
# sets the clock so this leg reports ABSOLUTE time; without it the second leg
# would restart at t=0, overlap the first, and any velocity measured across the
# join would be wrong.
#
# -initial_cond was dead code until 2026-09-15: it was parsed and stored and
# nothing ever called IGAReadVec. It now loads the vector and prints the phi
# range so a wrong file is obvious. The IGA must match the one that wrote the
# snapshot (same -Nx/-Ny/-p/-C/-dof/domain); IGAReadVec errors on a length
# mismatch, which catches the usual wrong-geometry mistake.
#
# Why continue rather than restart longer from scratch: the first leg spent most
# of its time relaxing the initial clipped disc into the equilibrium SHAPE. At
# 90 days theta=120 has essentially got there (velocity spread 0.27 of v_mean)
# while theta=60 has not (1.33). Continuing from the relaxed state is where the
# growth velocity becomes a clean steady quantity rather than a mixture of shape
# relaxation and growth.
#
# HEADROOM: at theta=60 the contact line is 2.08e-4 m wide in a 3.75e-4 m
# domain, leaving 8.3e-5 m (97 eps) to each vapour reservoir, closing at
# ~1.5e-12 m/s. That is ~660 days to contact, so 90 more days is safe -- but the
# growth rate will drift upward as the diffusion distance shortens, so v_n(t)
# here is not expected to be flat.
-t_start 7.776e6
# =============================================================================
-t_final 1.5552e7

-temp -20.0
-humidity 1.00
-grad_temp0 0.0,0.0,0.0                 # isothermal: theta is the only driver

# THERMAL reservoir, every face. Without this the box is insulated and latent
# heat shuts the experiment off almost immediately: batch 2026-09-14 measured a
# drift of +1.49e-4 K at theta=30 and -1.47e-4 K at theta=150, which is exactly
# the dT needed to move rho_vs(T) by the 1.41e-5 supersaturation that was
# driving growth. Depositing 1e-5 % of the ice releases that much heat, so the
# driving force annihilates itself and the ice changes by ~0.003 % in 90 days
# instead of tens of percent.
#
# With -grad_temp0 all zero, the solver pins T = temp0 on EVERY face (a uniform
# isothermal bath), which is what an open pore in a large regolith body sees.
# Unlike a vapour reservoir on the y walls, pinning T there is harmless: it does
# not feed ice at the contact line.
-flag_BC_Tfix 1

-beta_sub0 3.9704e6                     # K&P beta0 at -20 C (alpha_c = 2e-3)
-d0_sub0 1.0166e-9                      # physical capillary length at -20 C

# --- the substrate, as surface energies ---
-sigma_ia 0.109
-sigma_as 0.3
-sigma_is 0.3545000

# --- vapour reservoir, x-faces only, both at saturation ---
-flag_BC_rhovfix 1
-rhovfix_axis 0
-rhovfix_lo 1.000000
-rhovfix_hi 1.000000

-dtCFL 1
-dtCFL_dphimax 0.2
-outp 0
-n_out 60
